// The seam check: the real Go host in server mode, built from `OMNIVIEW_UI=next`, loading plugin bundles that were built OUTSIDE the host's tree
// (ui-next/plugins/fixture/build.mjs), in WebKit and Chromium. What it proves (docs/ui-next/slices/n19-plugin-seam.md, 1.4):
//
//   a  a bundle built by its own Vite config is fetched from `/_/plugins/<id>/assets/entry.js`, linked to the host's React and kit, and contributes a sidebar
//      entry and a page to the shell; the window opens it with no route (no URL parameter and no hash: the default screen is the plugins'); the page's list opens in the sort its definition names and sorts by the columns that
//      declare a key (K3a: the host's list page holds the sort and the kit's table does it, a plugin built outside the host's tree states it as data)
//   b  ONE React and ONE kit: the bundle's imports are the registry's instances, and `useTheme()` in the plugin reads the host's provider
//   c  the negative control: the same plugin with its own copy of the kit fails that read, and the check sees it fail
//   d  a page that throws is contained: the frame, the sidebar and the other pages stay; Retry tries again
//   e  a bundle of the old shell is refused before it runs (the global it sets is never set); a plugin with no bundle is a named state
//   g  the rail (S2a): one entry for each plugin that contributes (the healthy plugin and its copy; the refused ones have none), in the order each declares; choosing the other entry shows its
//      sidebar and closes no tab, activating a tab makes its plugin current; closing the last tab leaves the empty window (decision A17), which opens nothing by itself and offers the plugins to open
//   h  the bottom panel (P1a, P1b-3a): the host's own Logs tool comes first (empty: it says how a session arrives and has no `+`), then the tool each plugin that contributes brings, "Streams" and "Streams copy", in the order of the rail
//      (the two say the same `order`, so the rail's order tells them apart, whichever plugin finishes loading first: R2), drawn by the host's strip over the plugin's own body; the status bar's toggle opens the panel, the tool's `+`
//      opens its three streams (running, ended, failed), a hidden stream keeps running while another is on screen and while the panel is hidden, and the card of a stream links to its item, which opens in a tab of the window
//   h2 the bottom panel when the rail's first plugin finishes loading LAST (R2): its bundle is held while the copy's tool is on screen with a stream open in it, then let through; the strip is in the rail's order, and the tool, the stream
//      and the body the person is on do not change (the tab of the copy moves right by the width of the tab that arrived, as the mock's does)
//   i  the session (S3a): the window opens EMPTY when nothing was saved (decision A17: no page opens by itself); two tabs of two plugins, the rail on the second and the panel open on the second plugin's tool
//      at a height the keyboard chose come back after a reload of the page, and after the host's process is stopped and started again (the packaged app's restart, in server mode); a session whose plugin is not
//      installed keeps its tab and says so; a session of a newer version, and a text that is no session, are kept aside in a slot of their own and never deleted, and the window opens empty. The session lives in
//      the host's window service (`backend/window`: `GetState` and `SaveState`, persisted to `windows.json` in the state root), the one the packaged app uses
//   j  the command palette (C1b, host providers): the chord opens it from a control of the page and the focus is in its input; typing the name of a page the plugin's sidebar opens finds it; the footer says the
//      four open keys the tab model has and none for a window (Shift Enter is refused, with the reason); Enter opens the page in the tab on screen (smart open: the same plugin), Option Enter opens it here (the tab
//      on screen goes to another plugin's page), Command or Control Enter opens a background tab, Command Shift Enter a new tab on screen; Escape gives the focus back to the control that had it, and so does the Search
//      control of the top row; the homes of the plugins that did not load say Degraded; the host's commands are exactly the ones it can run (the tab commands, Toggle panel in a browser tab),
//      each with the chord it is bound to; a kind with no provider (Settings) says Nothing to show yet. SHOTS=<dir> writes `host-<engine>-<state>.png` of the palette in those states
//   k  a plugin's unload and the Logs sessions (P1b-3c, A21): the fixture plugin's interface is unloaded while two Logs sessions follow its item, one that failed on its own and one that streams (the window's plugin
//      store, reached through React's hooks, since nothing in the product unloads a ready plugin yet; the bundle's request is held so the plugin stays off): both tabs STAY, in the same view with the lines they had
//      read, the streams closed once (the plugin counts it); the one that streamed ENDED, the one that had failed is AS IT WAS (its own words and time); each card says the plugin was unloaded and its resource link and
//      Open button cannot be used; the bundle is let through and nothing resumed, the link works, and a new press of Logs opens a NEW session beside them; closing the tabs frees every listener and closes each stream
//      once (SEAM_SHOTS=<dir> writes WebKit's pictures: before the unload, after it, after the reload; SEAM_CONTROL=<name> runs k alone with one perturbation that its named assertion must see)
//   l  the plugins' providers in the command palette (C1c), on the real host with two plugins loaded from bundles built outside the host's tree: each declares a `match` provider and three `search` providers in its
//      `contributionV1` (plugins/fixture/src/palette.ts), quiet until the check makes the page's control. With it: a typed item name finds the rows of both plugins, each plugin's section headed by its name, the fast
//      provider's before the slow one's; Enter opens the plugin's page in the tab on screen, Command Enter in a background tab, Option Enter here (the tab goes to the other plugin's page), Command Shift Enter in a new
//      tab; a plugin's command runs with its argument and the panel handle the host gave it; a provider that rejects is said in the band with a Retry (the plugin's words are not on screen); a plugin whose interface is
//      unloaded while the palette is open and its slow request in flight loses its section, the request is told to stop, and the other plugin's section stays; when it is loaded again its section comes back with no key
//      pressed; a plugin that fails to load loses its section the same way and gets it back with its bundle. SHOTS=<dir> writes `host-<engine>-providers.png` and `host-<engine>-providers-error.png`
//   m  a Logs session is its plugin and its target (P1b-3d, A22): pressing the command of a session that streams shows it and the plugin is asked for no second feed (it counts them); a target with the title of another and
//      another scope (the fixture's `app` and its twin in the south yard) is a second tab, each of the two draws its scope, and the tab that is left draws none when the other closes (a lone session draws no scope); a
//      session that ended on its own and one that failed are not shown again, a press opens a NEW session beside each, told by its ordinal, and no two tabs are named alike; closing every tab frees every listener and closes
//      each feed once (SEAM_SHOTS=<dir> writes WebKit's `identity-one-session.png` and `identity-twins.png`; SEAM_CONTROL=<name> runs m alone with one perturbation that its named assertion must see, and that
//      assertion only: a page that drew no tab, or the wrong one, is a failure of its own and never a detection)
//   n  a plugin that stops reaches the window (PU1, A21): the fixture plugin gets a PROCESS (`fixture/process`) and a host of its own (the other scenarios' plugins have none), the window shows its page, its Logs
//      session and its tool, and the check asks the host for the plugin's pid (`processinfo`, never a pattern), makes the plugin unable to start again (its binary loses its execute bit) and kills that pid. The host
//      finds the process gone on its next health check (up to 30 s) and says so with `plugin/unloaded` (the frames of the window's event socket are read: the plugin's id and `crashed`, no other member); what the
//      window shows is read: the rail names the plugin stopped, with the cause in its tip, its sidebar and its tool leave, its page's tab says why with a Retry, and the Logs session STAYS, ended with "The plugin was
//      unloaded." in the same view with its lines; the host's recovery reloads the plugin (a second `plugin/unloaded`, `reloaded`) and fails, and the rail still says stopped (the first cause stands); with the binary
//      back and the plugin loaded again the host says it runs (`plugin/state_change`), and the window shows one of everything again with no reload: one rail entry, one tab on its page, one tool, the session as it was, and the
//      sidebar as the user left it (S3, demo finding D5): the group of the page on screen is closed by the user before the plugin is killed, and it is still closed when the plugin is back (a tree that remembered nothing would open
//      it again for the entry the page is on), and the plugin's hook is told that no group is open (SEAM_SHOTS=<dir> writes WebKit's `sidebar-after-restart-webkit-void-1440x900.png` of the window then).
//      SEAM_CONTROL=process runs n alone with every perturbation of its controls (PROCESS_CONTROLS) made in turn just before the assertion each must trip, and passes when each tripped its own (one kill serves all of them:
//      the host's health check is up to 30 s); SEAM_CONTROL=<one of them> makes that one only, and SEAM_REMOVE=<one of them> leaves that control's assertion out, which must let its perturbation pass undetected
//   o  a page command that fails is a toast (O1b-1): on the item's detail page the fixture's Restart rejects as the runtime's hooks do (`window.__fixtureDetail`); the host's toast says `Timed out` with its one sentence and a Retry,
//      is an alert, is sticky (it is still there after 9 s, when a timed one would be gone) and takes no focus; F6 reaches it and Escape closes it and gives the focus back to where it was; a failure that is not the host's says
//      `Operation failed` and none of its text (a password in it is nowhere in the document); a cancelled command makes no toast; a failure that comes back after its toast was dismissed is the same record and its toast says `Timed out 2 times`,
//      and Retry makes the third: `Timed out 3 times` (each wait is on a state that lasts, never on the frame between them: O1b-2, status/O1b-2.md)
//
//   p  a plugin whose load fails reaches the window and can be retried (PU2, PU1-01 and PU1-02): the same host and fixture process as n, whose NEXT START the check makes fail with a file next to its binary (`start-mode`:
//      `exit` ends the process before it says it is ready, `hang` keeps it silent). The host is asked to reload the plugin (`ReloadPlugin`, as a rebuild does) and its load fails: the frames of the window's event socket are
//      read (`plugin/unloaded`: `reloaded`, then `failed`, the plugin's id and the cause and no other member, and no phase of Running); the rail names the plugin that did not start, with the host's sentence in its tip,
//      its tab says why with a Retry and draws no page and no spinner, the host kept a record of the plugin (it is listed as Failed, so Retry has something to retry), and a window that is opened then, which the host told nothing,
//      learns it from the inventory and shows the plugin failed from the first. The Retry asks the host: the plugin starts again, fails
//      again, and the window is back in the failed state, never in a spinner. With the process told to hang, Retry shows the plugin starting again (a spinner, no Retry to press twice) for as long as the host waits, and the
//      load ends as failed with the cause `timeout` within the loader's own start timeout (15 s); the sentence says it took too long. With the file gone, Retry runs the plugin: the host says it runs, and the window shows
//      one of everything again with no reload (SEAM_SHOTS=<dir> writes WebKit's pictures of the five states). The same process then speaks ANOTHER PLUGIN PROTOCOL (S4): the fixture's manifest says `sdk_protocol_version: 2`, the SDK's own override, so
//      the real process serves protocol 2 and the host refuses it by the number the handshake negotiated: `plugin/unloaded` says `incompatible` with the two numbers and no other member, the rail says update required, the tab says the host's sentence
//      and both versions and draws NO Retry (asking again cannot help) and no page, the host lists the plugin as Failed with the same two numbers, and a window opened then shows the same from the inventory; with the manifest put back the host
//      reloads the plugin and it runs again with no reload. SEAM_CONTROL=failed-load runs p alone with every perturbation of its controls (FAILED_CONTROLS) made in turn just before the assertion each must trip; SEAM_CONTROL=<one of them>
//      makes that one only, and SEAM_REMOVE=<one of them> leaves that control's assertion out, which must let its perturbation pass undetected (the proof that each control trips its own assertion and no other)
//
//   q  the notification centre (O1b-2): the history a failed page command leaves, read from the bell, over the real host. A failure whose toast the person DISMISSES is read (the model: S81): the bell has no dot (its name is
//      exactly `Notifications`), the centre opened from the bell lists the record, read, the focus is on its row's title, and Escape closes it and gives the focus back to the bell. A failure whose toast is NOT dismissed is an
//      unread record (`Notifications, 1 unread, 1 error`); opening the centre takes the toast out of the layer (one presenter: the stack waits while the centre is open) and a failure that arrives while it is open is in the
//      list with no toast; closing it with Escape after more than 1 s reads what it listed (the bell is `Notifications` again, no toast comes back, the focus is on the bell). After a RELOAD, once the window service holds the
//      history (the first time the history is read from it), the window restores it: the bell has no dot, the restored records raised no toast and no announcement, and a restored record's Retry is not offered: its row says
//      `Retry is unavailable. Not available after restart.`
//
//   s  Settings (ST1) over the real host, in the sheet and in the Settings window: a host of its own with no plugins (Settings needs the host's settings service, its window service and a state root nothing else wrote), six independent
//      parts, then the watch over errors that covers them. The sheet (what a browser has in place of the native window): the chord, the gear and the palette's command open it with the host's own rows; a toggle applies at once
//      and is still applied after a reload; a value the host refuses (`developer.pprof_port` 70000) says the host's words in its lane and the host keeps what it had; Escape closes it and the focus goes back. The window
//      (`/?screen=settings-window`, what the native window of kind `settings` draws): no shell, no dialog, a caption with Close; `OpenSettings` names its page and shows it, `HideSettings` hides it, a show again asks the host what it
//      holds now, and Escape, the close chord and Close hide it by asking the host. The two together: a change in one reaches the other within 2 s through the host's `settings/changed`, and a draft typed in the sheet survives a
//      change of another setting made in the window. The events connection (a window page that is looked at all the while): its socket is dropped, a setting is changed from the host before the runtime opens another one, and the page shows the host's value once it is back, and follows the next change. The slots: 100 window pages opened and closed one after another all read their rows, and the window service lists the windows it listed before (the realm refuses a 64th LIVE
//      window and retires a closed page after about 3.4 s: INV2). The secrets (the host declares three, in Telemetry): one is saved through the sheet, is said to be saved after a reload, is cleared, and its value is never in the document,
//      in the host's snapshot or in the host's log. The popups: a tooltip inside the open sheet is shown above it, and a native select takes type-ahead. The log: no page error and no settings error in the host's log.
//      SEAM_CONTROL=settings runs s alone with every control of SETTINGS_PARTS made in turn; SEAM_CONTROL=<name> makes one, in the part it belongs to alone; SEAM_REMOVE=<name> leaves that control's assertion out, and its perturbation must then pass undetected
//   t  the Plugins page (PL1): the host's own page for the plugins that are installed, opened as a person opens it (the palette's command Plugins, no hash), on a host of its own whose fixture plugin has a PROCESS (as in n and p) and whose copy has none, and in a
//      second page, which is a second window in server mode, opened the same way and never reloaded. The list is the host's inventory (`ListPlugins`: the rows, their versions and their order, and the count the heading and the footer say). Each action
//      is read from the host's EVENTS, in the host's order, with the page's own call held on its way to the host (the page says that the work is going on and nothing of its result) or on its way back (the host has done it and told its events,
//      and the row has followed them with the call still open). RELOAD: `plugin/unloaded` (reloaded), then `plugin/state_change` (Running); the row and the header show the work and Starting again; the rail agrees. DISABLE: the row says Disabled
//      from `plugin/enablement_changed`. ENABLE is two host calls (`SetPluginEnabled(true)`, then `ReloadPlugin`): the half-way state is read, and so is the end of an Enable whose second call fails (the binary has lost its execute bit): the plugin is on
//      and did not start, and the row, the rail and the host's own record say so in the host's words and never in its text (the raw answer, the crash's error and the reasons of the events are nowhere in either window), with a sticky notice of the
//      host and a Retry that starts the plugin and does not turn it on again. KILL: the pid the host names (never a pattern), the row says Stopped with the host's sentence for a crash and offers Retry, the host gives up its recovery and the cause stands, and
//      Retry from the page asks the host and the plugin runs. UNINSTALL, behind the browser's own confirm (the page's words for what it erases and keeps; Cancel and Escape close it, give the focus back to the control that opened it and call nothing of
//      the host): the row and the rail entry leave on `plugin/unloaded` (uninstalled) with the call still open, the plugin's directory is gone, and the second window loses the row with no reload. SEAM_SHOTS=<dir> writes WebKit's
//      `plugins-page-<state>-webkit-void-1440x900.png`. SEAM_ONLY=plugins-page runs t alone; SEAM_CONTROL=plugins-page runs it alone with every perturbation of its controls (PAGE_CONTROLS) made in turn just before the assertion each must trip (one run
//      serves them all); SEAM_CONTROL=<one of them> makes that one only, and SEAM_REMOVE=<one of them> leaves that control's assertion out, which must let its perturbation pass undetected
//
//   u  the connections (HM1, HM2), over the real host, on a host of its own whose plugin `fixture-f-conn` has a PROCESS that serves two connections, each with a `Description` (`north`, `Fixture · region one`, starts in about 300 ms; `south`, `Fixture · region two`,
//      starts as the file `connection-mode` next to the plugin's binary says: none, `unauthorized` or `unreachable`, which the host answers with a 422). Windows with nothing saved, in this order. E, F, G (HM2: rule R4 while the list of connections is UNREAD,
//      the window's first read, `GetAllConnectionStates`, held on its way to the host by a hold made before the page is opened): E, Home says Reading connections… and the plugin's entry is pressed in the empty window; nothing opens while the list is held, then the
//      press is answered with a Home tab and no Overview tab (two connections, none used); F, a link `#/<plugin>/items` that names no connection does the same; G, the held read ends as a network that failed: the link opens no tab, the empty window says the
//      connections could not be read, a press on the plugin's entry opens no tab of the plugin, and Retry reads the list again (the line goes, Home lists both). A: the empty window is Home and lists both connections as the host says them (Disconnected: nothing
//      was started), each with the plugin's description as the second line of its row (and the host lists it); rule R4: the plugin's entry, with two connections and none used, opens a Home tab and no Overview tab, and on Home it moves the focus to the plugin's
//      connections; Home starts north (the start is held on its way: Connecting… on the row and no tab) and opens it in a tab (Overview, the plugin's home since HM3; the chip is named Connection: north and the rows of its table are north's) and says Connected; the chip's picker lists north (current,
//      Connected) and south (Disconnected) with the description under each name, type-to-filter says `No matches for "zz"`, Enter on south says Connecting… on its row and then the picker closes with the focus on the chip, the chip and the rows are south's, no tab
//      opened and Back does not return to north (the history entry was replaced). RECENT (HM2): Home's Recent lists south and then north, the connections used last, most recent first, and draws no section of the others; the host holds the tabs, a reload of the
//      page reads them again, and Recent still lists south first (it is made from the tabs' history: nothing new is saved, and north, which the replaced entry no longer says, is among the others). THE ROW MENU (HM2): Shift+F10 on a focused row opens the kit's menu
//      (Open, Open in new tab, Copy name); Copy name makes Home's live region say Copied <name>; Open in new tab opens a NEW tab in the connection (one more, on screen, its chip named after it); a row whose start is held is connecting and its Open and Open in new
//      tab are refused on the items with the reason, Copy name is not. B: a Logs session started from a row of the Items list (opened from the overview by the link of a row) through the host's panel handle (decision A21) is still in the panel after the tab's connection changed, its card says Streaming and its lines
//      keep coming, and closing it frees the plugin's feed. O (HM3, the overview): a connection chosen from Home lands on the plugin's home, which is the overview, in ONE tab titled Overview and named after the connection; its one heading is the connection's NAME and
//      never its key, its path (above the heading and in the status bar) is the plugin and the connection, the sidebar's entry of the overview is the current one, and the page draws a section of each of the three kinds the plugin gave it (a list, a table, key and
//      value pairs); the link of a row of the list is an anchor that opens the Items list in this tab, the sidebar's entry brings the overview back, the header's Refresh asks the plugin once, and the chip's choice of south keeps the page: the overview of south, with its own
//      heading and table (SEAM_SHOTS=<dir> writes WebKit's `overview-landing`, `overview-items` and `overview-south` pictures, `-webkit-void-1440x900.png`). C: the palette has a row for each connection (`Connection: south, Seam Connections · Disconnected`), found by its name, `switch` and `connection`; Enter has the host start it (the row is inert
//      meanwhile) and moves the tab on screen to it; the command Home ranks first for `home` and opens a new Home tab each time. D: a start the host refuses (south, `unauthorized`) says Needs sign-in and the host's sentence on the picker's row, which has no
//      description then (the picker stays open, the tab stays on north, choosing north closes it), on Home's row (no tab opens) and in the palette (its footer names the row and the reason, the row is inert while the host works and not after, Escape closes it with
//      the window as it was); the failure is every window's (HM2, PT2): another window, opened before and left on Home, says Needs sign-in and the host's sentence on the row of south with no action of its own and no StartConnection request, and Connected once
//      the start works, and a window opened after the failure says the same from the host's list alone; with the file gone the same choice starts south and goes to it, and `unreachable` says Not responding with its sentence. Nothing in the window can stop a
//      connection, so the check stops south as the host's operator (`StopConnection`) before each part that needs a start; the failed starts are the 422s of the allow-list (a held read that ends as a network that failed puts nothing on the wire for it to
//      name), and a read of the host's list that overlaps a start is told to ask again (`RESET_REQUIRED`, `restartQuery`), which the check does. The plugin's process ends with the host's. SEAM_ONLY=connections runs u alone; SEAM_CONTROL=connections runs it
//      alone with every perturbation of its controls (CONNECTIONS_CONTROLS) made in turn just before the assertion each must trip (one run serves them all); SEAM_CONTROL=<one of them> makes that one only, and SEAM_REMOVE=<one of them> leaves that control's
//      assertion out, which must let its perturbation pass undetected
//
//   v  the Problems and Operations tools of the host's panel (PT1), over the real host, on a host of its own whose two plugins (`fixture-a-ok`, which is the one that is killed, and `fixture-f-conn`) have a PROCESS that reports activity when the check appends
//      a line to the file `activity-script` next to its binary (`fixture/activityscript`: `task begin|step|finish`, `alert fire|resolve`). OPERATIONS: a task is reported at 1 of 4 and at 3 of 4 items and finishes (Running with the plugin's own count, then
//      Succeeded and no dot); a task that fails while Problems is on screen puts the dot `Operations, 1 unread failure` on its tab, which stays until the Operations tool has shown the row, when the window marks the record read (one call) and the host holds the
//      mark. PROBLEMS: an alert is a row and a dot while it fires and goes when the plugin resolves it; a connection that does not answer (started by the check's own call of the host, as an operator would: the host refuses it and tells every window, with the error in its public
//      words and none of the plugin's own) is a row and the tab counts an error, and choosing it asks the host to start it again, which is refused again, opens nothing, leaves the row and says so in a sticky error toast (`Couldn't open south`, with the refusal's sentence) that the check closes, and with the file gone it starts the connection, opens its page and the row goes; a plugin killed by the pid the host names is a row, the tab counts two errors, its alert stays listed as Not confirmed and its running task
//      says Outcome unknown (the host no longer knows); choosing the row opens the plugin's page in one tab, which says why and offers Retry; Retry (RetryFailedPlugin, once the host has given up its own recovery) runs the plugin again, the row goes, the alert
//      stays Not confirmed until the plugin resolves it, and it does. Both tools are reached with the keyboard (the arrows and Enter on the tool tabs, the control key to the table and to the open button of a row, Enter on it). SEAM_ONLY=problems-operations
//      runs v alone; SEAM_CONTROL=problems-operations runs it alone with every control of PT_CONTROLS made in turn: each plants a fault that the assertion it is named for must see, with its own words (the report, the choice or the key is not made yet; or, where one act makes
//      several assertions true, the kill, the refused choice and the connection that answers, a wrong attribute or a decoy once the act is done); SEAM_CONTROL=<one of them> makes that one only, and SEAM_REMOVE=<one of them> leaves that control's assertion out, which must let its fault pass
//      undetected by every other assertion of the act. The Logs control `page-error` is not v's: the control of v that reads the page's errors is `pt-page-error`
//
//   The terminal scenarios (r to r8) drive the Terminal tool over the TERMINAL CHANNEL (docs/ui-next/terminal-channel-protocol.md): a terminal is reached by a capability and by one stream, a WebSocket at `/wails/stream/ws` that the check records frame by frame (Playwright's
//   `page.on('websocket')`), and by the calls `CreateTerminal`, `ChannelTerminalBusy`, `ListChannelTerminals`, `CloseChannelTerminal` and `DismissChannelTerminal`. No terminal is listed by the host, so the check learns each capability where the page does (the answer of its
//   `CreateTerminal`), never prints it, and reads what a terminal printed from what the streams carried. SEAM_TERMINAL_ONLY=r3,r5 (and SEAM_TERMINAL_REPEAT=<n>) runs the ones it names alone. SEAM_SHELL=<path> runs them against another shell (the CI image has bash; a zsh
//   of a fresh home asks its new-user questions, which swallow the first characters typed).
//
//   r  the Terminal tool on the host's own shell (T1, T2a, T3). Before the first terminal the emulator's code is not requested; `New terminal` starts a shell of this machine (the host lists exactly one terminal), the code is fetched then, the emulator takes the keyboard and a
//      command with a known output is run: the output line of `echo SEAM_$((6*7))` is in what the stream carried, and not only its echo. The session's tab is called by the shell (what the host answered to the call that started it, checked against what the shell says it is)
//      and never Terminal, the emulator's input is `Terminal input, <name>` and the tool's own tab stays Terminal. The terminal starts at the size the person sees: the call that starts it (a channel terminal, no page key) carries the rows and columns of the screen, the pty has
//      them and they are the grid the emulator itself counts, and no resize frame follows it; then the panel is raised with the End key and dragged shorter with the pointer, and each new size goes in a resize frame. The page's input, size and acknowledgements are frames of
//      one stream (the first frame is its one attach, the first ack is for nothing and comes after `ready`) and it makes no call of the events lane for a terminal. A keyboard user can always leave: Escape stays in the shell, F6 moves the focus out of it, and on a Mac Command J
//      hides the panel. Flow control, which the host paces the shell by (the page acknowledges every byte it parses, shown or hidden): a flood of about 8 MB completes in the terminal that is on screen and the `ack` frames are the bytes of the flood; a flood of 50 MB completes with
//      the panel HIDDEN (the shell waits for a file the check makes once the panel is hidden): the shell is back at its prompt, the page acknowledged what it parsed, shown again the end is in the emulator, and the shell answers a new command. A terminal that runs `sleep 60` asks when it is
//      closed (`Terminate session?`), Cancel and Escape keep the terminal and the process, Terminate session ends both with one CloseChannelTerminal; a terminal at its prompt closes with no question and one CloseChannelTerminal (the page asking the host once what runs in it). A second
//      terminal that runs `exit 3` is an ENDED session: its lane says `Exited with code 3`, the host keeps its end until the page dismisses it (one DismissChannelTerminal, no close, no question). No capability is in an address or on the console.
//   (r2 below r: the same terminal against the programs of the machine, `checkTerminalPrograms`, docs/ui-next/status/T1.md section 14.)
//
//   r3  a terminal outlives a reload of its page (T2b, `checkTerminalReattach`). The window's state holds the saved tabs (each tab's terminal id) and each browser tab keeps its capabilities in its own sessionStorage; a page that loads reads them from there, asks the host for the terminals of its capabilities (ListChannelTerminals) and takes its saved tabs' terminals back, each on one stream that attaches
//      with no offset. A reload in the middle of a session: the tab is back, the scrollback holds the markers, `echo $$` prints the same pid, typing works (input frames of the new stream), no CreateTerminal; a terminal its page left (a navigation) is held by the host as nobody's on the
//      lease it was made with and a return takes it back; two terminals come back in their order and closing one ends its shell. A reload in the middle of a flood of 3,000,000 numbered lines (about 26 MB): the end arrives, the lines the emulator holds count up by one and the page acknowledged
//      the bytes of output frames (replay included, never more); a slow stream across a reload is read again and again, every reading counting up by one at the join; a runaway producer (`yes`) is read on after a reload and stopped by Control C. A full-screen program (the alternate screen of a
//      program of the shell's own, one whose start has left the host's 8 MiB ring, and vim when the machine has it) is on the alternate screen with its text after the reload, and its end gives the shell back with its earlier output. The panel comes back as it was left.
//   r4  one stream holds a terminal at a time, and it does not outlive the host or its lease (T2b, `checkTerminalTakeover`). A second page of the same browser takes the terminal over: the host sends the first page's stream `taken-over` after the second's attach, the first says `Taken over by
//      another tab` and that its input is off, sends no input, no call and no frame; the second page's terminal goes on running and takes input after the first is closed. A restart of the host over the same state root: the saved tab comes back ended, says the earlier output is gone, and no stream
//      was answered and no terminal was started. A lease that runs out (a terminal of 20 s that the check hands to the page through the window's slots, let go when the page navigates away): the host ends it and keeps nothing of it, the capability opens nothing, and a page that loads later
//      finds the tab ended with the earlier output gone.
//   r5  a terminal whose taker has gone, and a terminal across a cut of its stream (T2d, `checkTerminalFollowups`). Take it back: a second page takes the terminal over (the first says `Taken over by another tab` and offers no way back while the taker is there); the second closes, and the first
//      (told by the host on its own stream, `released`, the moment the taker's stream ends: nothing is asked, nothing polls) says that the tab that took the terminal has closed and that the shell keeps running until its lease ends, and a `Take it back` button appears, which the keyboard reaches with Tab and presses with Enter in one round and the pointer clicks in the other:
//      the same shell runs again in the same tab on one new stream, after ListChannelTerminals. A tab that takes the terminal back must not displace another: the press asks the host and opens no stream when a third tab holds the terminal. A page whose events socket is away (a TCP proxy cuts it)
//      loses nothing of the terminal's business: it is told `released` on its stream while its events socket is away. A cut of the terminal stream in the middle of a numbered stream: the page connects again by itself with `from_offset` at the last byte it had, the host answers from there with nothing lost, every reading counts up by one across the join; an outage of
//      5 s ends the same way; one that outlasts the page's three tries (250 ms, 1 s, 4 s) makes the feed fail in words and a reload takes the same shell back; a shell the host ended while the page was away (killed by its pid) is an ended tab, and the page does not attach in a loop.
//   r6  a terminal across the cuts of the page's two sockets (the review of #677, B1, `checkTerminalReconnect`): the events socket cut for 1 s and for 5 s, then the terminal stream for 1 s and for 5 s. The same pid, a line typed during each cut (also when it is typed before the page has seen its stream close: the page keeps what the host has not said it applied and sends the rest after `ready`) and one after it reach the shell once and in order, no call of the
//      page answered not-found or FORBIDDEN and no frame was a `not-found`, and the shell can still be ended. Controls: RECONNECT_CONTROLS.
//   r7  two pages and nothing of one reaches the other (`checkTerminalIsolation`): a canary printed in the first terminal is in no frame or event of the second page; the 13 bare-id bindings named with the first terminal's id answer `omniview:session/not-found` from the second page and from callers that are no
//      window; a stream opened with a wrong capability gets exactly one `0x86` frame. The refusals of the second page's own calls are allowed for that phase only (RUNTIME_ALLOW). Controls: ISOLATION_CONTROLS.
//   r8  find in the scrollback, the context menu and the links of the Terminal tool (T3a, `checkTerminalFindMenu`), over the real host and the engine's own keyboard and pointer. A known output (200 numbered lines, the word in other cases and as part of other words, three marker lines) is printed through the real shell and the find is opened by the platform's chord (Command F on a Mac, Control F and Control Shift F elsewhere; the other platform's chord opens nothing):
//      its counts are those of a reference scan of the same rows (plain, Match case, Match whole word, a regular expression, `Invalid pattern`, `No results`), Enter, Shift+Enter, the Next and Previous buttons and F3 step by one and wrap, the match the count points at is the one the screen has selected, Escape and the Close button give the keyboard back to the shell, opening and closing the bar changes no size and sends no resize, the code of the find is requested only
//      once a find is made, a find after a reload finds a line of the replayed scrollback, and with 1,000,000 lines flooding in the bar answers the pointer (while the shell has not printed its last line), counts right at the end and the shell still takes what is typed (more than 20,000 matches say `20,000+`). The context menu opens on a right click, Shift+F10 and the Menu key (Copy not available with nothing selected and saying why, Paste, Select all, Clear, Find: each does what it says, the clipboard is the page's own stub), Escape closes it and gives the keyboard back, and a program that has the mouse gets
//      the right click and no menu opens unless Shift is held. A web address in the output says `Follow link (cmd + click)` (`ctrl` elsewhere) when the pointer is on it and is followed by that modifier and a click as exactly one call of the runtime's `Browser.OpenURL` (the Browser object, method 0, the address as its argument), which the check intercepts and answers 200 so that the host never opens a browser on this machine; a plain click, a `javascript:` line and a `file:` line make none. The same for a hyperlink the program writes (OSC 8, printed by the real shell: the words show one address, the program names another): the hover, a plain click that makes no call, the modifier and a click that makes one, and a `javascript:` and a `file:` hyperlink that make none; and no link ever calls `window.open` or asks a question (`confirm`, `alert`, `prompt`: the page's own are watched and no dialog opens). No request fails in the phase.
//
//   w  the Ports tool (PF1, PF2, PF3, `checkPorts`), over the real host, on a host of its own whose plugin `fixture-g-ports` (Seam Ports) has a PROCESS that serves one connection (`harbor`) and forwards a local port to a TCP server of its own (it sends `PF1-FIXTURE-HELLO` to every
//      connection and sends back what it is sent). One window with nothing saved, opened at the plugin's page before its connection is started: the tool, which has not been on screen, holds no handler of the host's port-forward events and asks for no list of forwards, though the
//      host's operator starts the connection (`StartConnection`) and the window hears it (`connection/status`). The press of the page's button asks the host once (`StartResourcePortForwardingSession`, in the page's own plugin and connection) and the page says
//      `Forward session <id> on 127.0.0.1:<local>` (the host and the port that the row's mapping says); the Ports tool is revealed with exactly one row, the forward the host lists (its answer states the host its plugin listens on, `local_host`, the loopback): its resource, the mapping `127.0.0.1:<local> → :8080/TCP`, the connection by its NAME (its id is
//      nowhere on screen) and Running, with one handler for each of the five host events it follows (and one more for `closed`, which the window's count of the forwards its pages started listens to while it holds one) and one call for the list. A client of the check's own (Node's `net`) connects to `127.0.0.1:<local>`: the first line is `PF1-FIXTURE-HELLO` and a line it writes comes back.
//      Open in browser is one call of the runtime's `Browser.OpenURL` (the Browser object, method 0, `http://127.0.0.1:<local>`), which the check intercepts with `page.route` and answers 200 so that the host never opens a browser on this machine, and never `window.open`, a question or a window of the browser; Copy address puts `127.0.0.1:<local>` on the clipboard (the page's own stub) and says `Address copied`;
//      the row's menu (a right click, Shift+F10) offers the same three and runs them, one at a time (what each item caused is read from where each record stood just before it was chosen: Copy writes the address once and asks the runtime to open nothing, Open makes one call that opens it and writes nothing to the clipboard, and neither uses window.open, a question or a window of the browser; the menu's Stop is the one that
//      stops the second forward below). A reload brings the row back from the host's list as it was, with one call for the list, and a second forward started from the page is a second row
//      that the host's `created` event made, with no call for the list. The page's second button asks for the local port its last answer gave: the host cannot give it twice and refuses it (a 422 that RUNTIME_ALLOW names, by the call), the page says `Refused PROVIDER_ERROR:` and one plain line, and nothing else changes: no second row, nothing more in the host's list, no `created` event, and the forward that
//      holds the port still carries. The page's Stop ends a forward the page started (the page says `Stopped forward <id>`, the row and the host's list lose it and its port refuses a connection), and a second press says the same and asks the host nothing. Stop in the tool: with the call held on its way the row says Stopping, its Stop waits and a second press asks the host nothing; with the host's answer
//      held after it closed the forward, the row leaves with the host's `closed` event, the host no longer lists it and its local port refuses a connection; with no forward left the tool says so.
//      THE HOST'S RULES (PF2), after a reload that gives the page and the window's count of its forwards a fresh start: a forward starts from a user action only, so the page's fourth button, which waits longer than the five seconds that an action's activation lasts and asks from a timer, is refused FORBIDDEN in the host's words and asks the host nothing (nothing is asked of the page while it waits: an evaluation or a wait
//      for an element would renew the activation), and a click still starts one; an id of a mebibyte is refused INVALID_REQUEST before anything leaves the page (no request over 64 KiB); one press that asks 1,000 forwards at once starts as many as the cap of 64 for the plugin in the connection leaves room for, refuses the rest RESOURCE_LIMIT with the cap as its limit and the host's words, the host lists exactly 64
//      sessions and no more start calls reach it than forwards started (how long the burst takes is recorded, not asserted); then the Ports tool lists what the host lists, without error, the plugin's process still runs, the page answers a press within two seconds and one more forward is refused with the cap's words; Stop all of them returns the host's list to what it was, every local port of the burst
//      refuses a connection and a new forward starts again. With the panel hidden the tool holds no handler of the host's events, and a forward started then shows it again. Then the plugin's process is killed by the pid the host names (its binary loses its execute bit, so that the host's recovery cannot start it again; the signal is a precondition that no control perturbs and no SEAM_REMOVE leaves out: after the binary lost its
//      execute bit (the reading does not depend on it) the host names the pid again, with the start identity it had and the binary this run installed, and nothing is awaited between that reading and the signal): the host finds it on its next health check (up to 30 s) and the row says Stopped and `Its plugin stopped.`, its Stop is disabled with that reason and a press asks the host nothing, Open in browser and Copy address are
//      disabled (the forward must be running), and its local port refuses a connection. After the host has gone every local port the scenario saw a forward on refuses a connection (`listener-left`). What the check itself fails at is the scenario's: no request fails in the phase (the phase's own guard, whose control is runtime-4xx), no route of the check fails (a held call that cannot be let go is kept with the
//      engine's own error), and the page raises no error but the one a reload makes of a call it cuts (only while the page is being replaced, and only a message that says a call was cut). SEAM_ONLY=ports runs w alone; SEAM_CONTROL=ports runs it alone with every perturbation of its controls (PORTS_CONTROLS) made in turn just before the assertion each must trip (one run serves them all: the host finds a killed process
//      on its next health check, up to 30 s); SEAM_CONTROL=<one of them> makes that one only, and SEAM_REMOVE=<one of them> leaves that control's assertion out, which must let its perturbation pass undetected
//
//   The failed requests (INV1): every request any page of the run makes that answers 4xx or 5xx is recorded with the phase it happened in, and the phase fails unless each is in RUNTIME_ALLOW (an explicit list: the entry, its reason, the phases it
//   is expected in). SEAM_CONTROL=runtime-4xx plants one 422 in a clean page and passes when the phase's assertion saw it; SEAM_REMOVE=runtime-4xx leaves that assertion out, and the planted 422 must then pass undetected
//
//   Every scenario starts from a window with nothing saved: the host keeps ONE session for its main window, and a page that a scenario left behind would be restored by the next.
//
//   node ui-next/plugins/e2e/seam.check.mjs        needs bin/Omniview-server (OMNIVIEW_UI=next task build:server) and Playwright's browsers; ENGINES=webkit or ENGINES=chromium runs one engine
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
